{
  "dom_id": "step-q250076",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The parabola $y={x}^{2}+2x-3$ and the line $y=5x+7$ intersect at the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2}),$ where ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2}.$ Find the values of ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}.$\n\n${y}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{y}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "We need to solve the following system consisting of the equations of the curves:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y={x}^{2}+2x-3 \\\\ y=5x+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the equations equal to one another and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-3 & =5x+7 \\\\ {x}^{2}-3x-10 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)(x-5) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}_{1}<{x}_{2},$ the $x$-coordinates of the intersection points are ${x}_{1}=-2$ and ${x}_{2}=5.$\n\nTo find the $y$-coordinates of the intersection points, we plug these values into either curve. Let's choose the line since it's simpler.\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{1}=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =5{x}_{1}+7 \\\\  & =5(-2)+7 \\\\  & =-3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso one point of intersection is $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,-3).$\n\n- Substituting ${x}_{2}=5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =5{x}_{2}+7 \\\\  & =5(5)+7 \\\\  & =32,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the other point of intersection is $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(5,32).$\n\nA sketch of the situation is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14220347/assets/98d656438193d68da44573720da4a0fa5f7e1f7d253c29e0de2e309468c8b552.png)",
